WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.767
Book Three, Proposition Thirty-Three.

2
00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:09.619
On a given segment, to describe a segment of a circle admitting an angle equal to a given rectilineal angle.

3
00:00:09.619 --> 00:00:14.631
Let A B be the given segment and the angle at C be the given rectilineal angle.

4
00:00:14.631 --> 00:00:19.770
It is required to describe on A B a circle segment admitting an angle equal to C.

5
00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:26.906
At point A on A B, construct angle B A D equal to angle C by One Twenty-Three.

6
00:00:26.906 --> 00:00:31.556
From A, draw A E at right angles to D A by One Eleven.

7
00:00:31.556 --> 00:00:39.391
Bisect A B at F by One Ten, draw F G at right angles to A B meeting A E at G, and join G B.

8
00:00:39.391 --> 00:00:52.049
Because A F equals F B, F G is common, and the right angles at F are equal, triangles A F G and B F G are congruent by One Four, so A G equals G B.

9
00:00:52.049 --> 00:01:00.745
Construct circle G A, with center G and defining radius point A; it passes through B as circle A B E.

10
00:01:00.745 --> 00:01:01.520
Join E B.

11
00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:11.580
Because A D is drawn from the extremity A of diameter A E at right angles to A E, the line A D touches the circle A B E at A by the Porism to Three Sixteen.

12
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:24.249
Since the tangent A D touches the circle at A and the chord A B is drawn across from the point of contact, by Three Thirty-Two the angle D A B equals the angle A E B in the alternate segment of the circle.

13
00:01:24.249 --> 00:01:30.552
But angle D A B was constructed equal to the given angle C; therefore angle A E B also equals angle C.

14
00:01:30.552 --> 00:01:36.670
And if C is a right angle, the semicircle on A B admits a right angle directly by Three Thirty-One.

15
00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:44.580
Thus on the given segment A B, the circle segment A E B has been described admitting an angle equal to C, as was required to do.

16
00:01:45.000 --> 00:02:05.352
Proposition Three Thirty-Three is the constructive inverse of the Alternate Segment Theorem: by laying off the target angle C between the chord A B and a line A D that will become the tangent, the center G of the required circle is uniquely determined as the intersection of the normal to A D at A and the perpendicular bisector of A B.

17
00:02:05.352 --> 00:02:16.134
As Robert Simson noted, the acute and obtuse cases share this identical construction, while Clavius observed that the right-angle case reduces immediately to a semicircle on A B.

18
00:02:16.134 --> 00:02:27.400
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred forty-one synthesizes nineteen rules across Books One and Three into a single locus tool—the arc of constant subtended angle on a fixed chord.

